`["[N T]"]` is a fixed collection of `T` with maximum length `N`.

[codeblock, $code "moth":
    values [3 I32] = [10, 20]

    capacity #I32 = 4
    scratch ~[capacity String] = []
]

### Type identity

Fixed capacity is semantic type identity.

These are distinct incompatible types:

[codeblock, $code "moth":
    [I32]
    [4 I32]
    [8 I32]
]

A fixed type limits membership. A growable type has no declared capacity.

Direct assignment requires the same type. These pairs need explicit construction:

- from growable to fixed
- from fixed to growable
- between different fixed capacities

### Capacity syntax

Capacity in type position must be:

- a positive `I32` literal
- a bare visible `#I32` constant name

Capacity position is not general expression position.

Arithmetic, calls, field access, const-record projection, conditionals and
nested expressions are invalid there.

Name the calculation first:

[codeblock, $code "moth":
    base #I32 = 4
    larger_capacity #I32 = base + 2
    values ~[larger_capacity String] = []
]

### Proving a construction fits

Every fixed type states both its capacity and its element type. In value
position, `["[8]"]` constructs one integer. In type position it lacks an
element type.

[codeblock, $code "moth":
    append_last |prefix [3 I32], last I32| -> [4 I32] {
        return [prefix, last]
    }
]

The prefix can contribute at most three elements and `last` contributes one.
The result contains the actual items without padding. An unbounded runtime
splice cannot establish that it fits. Use checked insertion when the bound
depends on runtime data.

The compiler adds bounds for consecutive contributions and takes the maximum
over mutually exclusive branches. A nested head row counts once toward its
outer capacity. Finite constant ranges and collection loops with a known constant length or
fixed capacity have an upper bound on iterations. Multiply that bound by the
proven maximum per iteration. Loops, slots and wrappers must account for every
emission, including repeated slot uses. An unbounded source or an unknown
conditional loop cannot prove a maximum. Bounds use checked arithmetic. A known constant
length can supply a tighter bound than a sequence's capacity.

### Empty fixed values

An immutable binding cannot be initialised with an empty fixed literal because
there is no later mutable owner path to fill it.

These are valid:

- a mutable empty fixed binding
- a fixed collection field default with an explicit field type

A fixed literal may contain fewer items than its capacity.

Its logical length is the number of stored items, not the maximum capacity.

### Push and capacity

Fixed `push` appends after the last stored item. When the collection has a
free slot, the push succeeds and returns no value. When the collection is
full, the push fails, so a fixed push needs `catch` or postfix `!`:

[codeblock, $code "moth":
    values ~[3 I32] = [10, 20]

    ~values.push(30) catch {
        io.error("no free slot")
    }
]

The failure goes through the builtin `Error!` collection error path, and a
failed push leaves the stored items unchanged. The `~` before the call is
the explicit mutable receiver access that every mutating collection
operation requires.

`remove` frees one slot. Removing an item lets later pushes succeed again,
and it never changes the collection's capacity or type.
